<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kailua-Kona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kailua-Kona is a town on the west coast of the island of Hawaiʻi, in the Kona district. It was a royal centre of Hawaiian chiefs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> spent his last years there, dying at his residence of Kamakahonu in 1819. Huliheʻe Palace, a royal summer residence, and Mokuʻaikaua Church, founded in 1820 by the first Christian missionaries to the islands, stand in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kailua-kona.jpg" alt="Kailua-Kona" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Travisthurston · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kona_Mauka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
